.\" Man page for a Mosaico installation, installed by install.sh as
.\" /usr/local/share/man/man7/mosaico.7 — the installer either picks it up from
.\" its own directory or downloads this file from the repository.
.\"
.\" @SCRIPT_VERSION@ is substituted with the installer's version at install time.
.\"
.\" Every path here is duplicated from a constant in install.sh (DATA_DIR,
.\" SECRETS_DIR, QUADLET_DIR, CLI_WRAPPER, MOSAICOD_PORT, ...). Changing one of
.\" those constants means editing this page too; nothing checks that they agree.
.TH MOSAICO 7 "" "mosaico-installer @SCRIPT_VERSION@" "Mosaico"
.SH NAME
mosaico \- the Mosaico installation on this host: commands, files and removal
.SH DESCRIPTION
Mosaico runs as a system-level Podman container supervised by systemd through
Quadlet: \fBmosaicod\fR, the Mosaico daemon. Its PostgreSQL database runs
alongside it in a second container, \fBmosaico-postgres\fR, unless the
installation was pointed at a PostgreSQL instance of your own — see
\fBDATABASE\fR below. Everything was set up by \fBmosaico-installer\fR(8).
.PP
This page describes what the installer put on this machine. For the daemon
itself, its configuration keys and its API, see the online documentation at
https://docs.mosaico.dev.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mosaicod \fIcommand\fB ...
Host wrapper around the daemon CLI, installed at /usr/local/bin/mosaicod. It
forwards its arguments into the running \fBmosaicod\fR container, so it behaves
like a native binary. Run
.B mosaicod --help
for the commands the installed version supports.
.IP
The work needs root, because the containers belong to root's Podman and the
environment file is readable only by root, but you do not have to ask for it:
called from an unprivileged shell the wrapper re-executes itself through
\fBsudo\fR, so \fBmosaicod\fR works as an ordinary command and prompts for a
password if sudo asks for one. To drop that prompt, grant the command
\fINOPASSWD\fR in a \fI/etc/sudoers.d\fR entry. It forwards into a running
container, so it reports the container as missing when the daemon is down.
.TP
.B mosaico-installer \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
A copy of the installation script, kept at /usr/local/bin/mosaico-installer. Use
it to re-run the installation, change the configuration, upgrade to another image
tag or uninstall. Run
.B mosaico-installer --help
for the full list of options.
.SH SERVICE MANAGEMENT
The container is an ordinary systemd service, managed with the usual tools:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl status mosaicod.service
systemctl restart mosaicod.service
journalctl -u mosaicod.service -f
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same applies to \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR when the database is embedded.
Container-level output is available through \fBpodman logs mosaicod\fR.
.PP
The daemon listens on port 6726, on every address of the host.
.SH DATABASE
mosaicod keeps its metadata in PostgreSQL, reached through the
\fBMOSAICOD_DB_URL\fR connection string in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/mosaicod.env\fR. Which of the two arrangements below
is in use was recorded as \fBPG_MODE\fR in \fI/var/lib/mosaico/install.conf\fR.
.TP
.B embedded
The default. PostgreSQL runs as a second container, \fBmosaico-postgres\fR, on
the internal \fBmosaico.network\fR, and is not published to the host: only
mosaicod reaches it. The password is generated on the first run, kept in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/postgres_password\fR, reused by every later run and
never printed. The data lives in the \fBmosaico-postgres-data\fR Podman volume.
.TP
.B external
A PostgreSQL instance you already run, given to the installer with
\fB--pg-host\fR and friends. No database container is created and none is
supervised: the installer only writes the connection string. The database and the
role have to exist before the installation, and the role needs enough privilege
to create the schema mosaicod uses on first start. Backups, upgrades and
availability of that instance stay yours.
.PP
An external instance running on this same host cannot be reached at
\fI127.0.0.1\fR from inside the container — that address is the container's own
loopback — so the installer rewrites a loopback host to
\fIhost.containers.internal\fR, which Podman resolves to the host. For that to
work PostgreSQL must listen on an address other than the host loopback
(\fBlisten_addresses\fR in \fIpostgresql.conf\fR) and \fIpg_hba.conf\fR must
accept connections from the container network. Passing the host's LAN address as
\fB--pg-host\fR instead avoids the indirection altogether.
.PP
Re-running the installer with \fB--pg-host\fR against an installation that had
an embedded database stops the \fBmosaico-postgres\fR container and removes its
unit. The \fBmosaico-postgres-data\fR volume is deliberately left in place, so
nothing is lost and switching back reuses the old database.
.SH TLS
mosaicod does not issue certificates. Whatever their origin, the certificate
chain and the private key are copied to
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/cert.pem\fR and
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/key.pem\fR, mounted read-only into the container,
and read by the daemon only at startup.
.PP
When the installer obtained the certificate from Let's Encrypt, renewal is
automatic. The certbot timer shipped by the distribution does the renewing, and
\fI/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/mosaico.sh\fR copies each renewed
certificate into place and restarts the service. Check that both halves are in
order with:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl list-timers certbot.timer
sudo certbot renew --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Renewal repeats the same challenge as the first issuance, so \fBport 443 has to
stay open for the TLS challenge\fR. A firewall rule added after installation is
the usual reason a renewal that used to work stops working.
.PP
For a certificate supplied with \fB--tls-cert\fR and \fB--tls-key\fR no hook is
installed, and renewal is yours to perform: overwrite the two files and restart
the service.
.RS 4
.nf
sudo install -m 600 fullchain.pem /var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/cert.pem
sudo install -m 600 privkey.pem /var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/key.pem
sudo systemctl restart mosaicod.service
.fi
.RE
.SH API KEYS
When the installation requested a master API key, the daemon runs with key
enforcement on and every client must present one. The master key is stored in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/master_api_key\fR and has the \fBmanage\fR
permission, which covers reading, writing, deleting and managing other keys.
.PP
Further keys are managed through the daemon itself, for example:
.RS 4
.nf
mosaicod api-key create --permissions read
mosaicod api-key list
mosaicod api-key revoke \fIfingerprint\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without that request the daemon accepts unauthenticated clients on every
address of the host.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosaico/install.conf
Non-secret record of the choices made at install time, including the storage
backend and the storage directory in use, the database mode and, for an external
database, its host, port, database name and role.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosaico/secrets
Generated secrets, readable by root only: the daemon environment file, the TLS
material under \fItls/\fR, the PostgreSQL password and \fIpostgres.env\fR when
the database is embedded and, when requested, the master API key.
.TP
.I /etc/containers/systemd
Quadlet unit definitions (\fImosaicod.container\fR, \fImosaico.network\fR and,
for an embedded database, \fImosaico-postgres.container\fR) from which systemd
generates the services.
.TP
.I /opt/mosaico
Present only when the installer was run with \fB--sdk\fR: a Python virtual
environment holding the \fBmosaicolabs\fR SDK. Activate it with
\fB. /opt/mosaico/bin/activate\fR. Removed by \fB--uninstall\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/mosaico.sh
Present only when the certificate came from Let's Encrypt. Run by certbot after
a renewal, it refreshes the copies under \fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls\fR and
restarts the daemon. Removed by \fB--uninstall\fR; the certificate itself is left
in \fI/etc/letsencrypt\fR, since other services on the host may be using it.
.PP
Dataset frames live either in the local directory recorded as \fBFS_DIR\fR in
\fIinstall.conf\fR, or in the configured object store bucket.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is passed to the daemon through
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/mosaicod.env\fR, written by the installer. Re-running
\fBmosaico-installer\fR is the supported way to change it; the generated
PostgreSQL password and master API key are preserved across runs, and so are
hand-made edits to that file only until the next run overwrites them.
.PP
Only the options given to the installer are written to that file, so any
setting left out keeps the daemon's own default. The data retention values are
documented at https://docs.mosaico.dev/config/cleanup.
.PP
The image tag is pinned by the installer rather than tracking \fIlatest\fR,
because the generated command line and environment variables belong to one
mosaicod release. Upgrade by re-running the installer with
\fB--version\fR, not by pulling a newer image under the same unit.
.SH UNINSTALLING
To stop and remove the services, the containers, the Quadlet units, the two
commands and this man page, leaving all stored data in place:
.RS 4
.nf
sudo mosaico-installer --uninstall
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stored data is never touched by that command. To wipe it as well, remove the
secrets, the storage directory recorded as \fBFS_DIR\fR in \fIinstall.conf\fR
and, for an embedded database, the volume it lives in:
.RS 4
.nf
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/mosaico/secrets /var/lib/mosaico/install.conf
sudo rm -rf \fI<storage directory>\fR
sudo podman volume rm mosaico-postgres-data
.fi
.RE
.PP
The exact commands, with the storage directory filled in, are printed by
\fBmosaico-installer --uninstall\fR when it finishes. Data kept in an external
object store, or in an external PostgreSQL instance, must be removed there.
